WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:19.377
Episode four: putting work aside with stash, then a remote, to share the repository with a colleague.

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:29.785
Work in progress: a line in the list, not committed. And something urgent arrives, which has nothing to do with it.

00:00:29.885 --> 00:00:46.069
git stash puts the uncommitted changes on a shelf: the working copy is clean again, and stash list shows the shelf.

00:00:46.169 --> 00:00:55.679
The urgent change, committed on its own.

00:00:55.779 --> 00:01:13.236
stash pop takes the work back off the shelf, and the stash is empty again. Now it can be committed too.

00:01:13.336 --> 00:01:27.617
Now a remote. A Git server is just a bare repository: the history without a working copy. Here it is a directory; on GitHub or GitLab only the address changes.

00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:53.544
remote add gives it the name origin. push -u sends main and ties our branch to it. branch -a now shows remotes/origin/main too: our record of where the remote's main is.

00:01:53.644 --> 00:02:22.139
A colleague clones the remote: a complete copy, history included. They add a line and push it.

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:45.535
Back in our repository. fetch downloads what is new without touching our branch: status says we are one commit behind, and the log shows which one.

00:02:45.635 --> 00:03:01.652
pull is fetch plus merge: the colleague's line is in our list.

00:03:01.752 --> 00:03:11.600 line:6%
Next episode: rebase, tags, and how to get back a commit you thought lost.
